          you want the i-Run you used in Europe, this guide sorts out which Reebok is which and where
          each can actually be bought and serviced. For the FR20z Floatride specifically, our
          full review goes deeper.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Reebok Treadmills: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Reebok treadmills are designed and sold under licence by RFE International, a UK
            company, and they are sold mainly in the UK, Europe, Australia and Asia; in the US they
            are hard to find new.</strong> Reebok’s official US equipment store described Reebok
            Fitness equipment as now available in the US on 14 October 2026, but listed no
            treadmills, and Amazon’s US catalogue showed no Reebok treadmill. US retailers such as
            Walmart and Academy have carried the FR30z and other models, but we could not confirm
            their stock.
          </p>
          <p>
            The current range centres on the FR20z and FR30z Floatride treadmills, the GT40z and Jet
            folding treadmills, the compact i-Run models and the Z-Tech machines, with the selection
            varying by country. Older Reebok treadmills sold in the US in the 2000s — the RT, V, R
            and ZigTech models among them — were made by ICON Health & Fitness, the company behind
            NordicTrack and ProForm, under an earlier licence.
          </p>
          <p>
            For American readers the practical advice is simple. Buy a Reebok treadmill only from a
            US seller who can show it is a 120-volt unit with a warranty honoured in the US, and
            otherwise choose a comparable machine with local stock and service. Our
            <a href="/reebok-fr20z-floatride-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Reebok FR20z review</a>
            covers the most searched model in detail, and our
            <a href="/best-cushioned-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioned treadmill roundup</a>
            lists US-stocked machines built around the same idea.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-them" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Makes Reebok Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Reebok itself is a sportswear brand, owned since 2022 by Authentic Brands Group. Its
            treadmills come from licensees, and there have been two main ones.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Today: RFE International</h3>
          <p>
            The official Reebok Fitness product catalogue, which lists the current treadmills, bikes
            and cross trainers and publishes support numbers by country, states at the foot of every
            page that it is operated by RFE International Ltd., United Kingdom. Trade coverage from
            2016 described RFE International as the licensee behind Reebok Fitness and reported that
            it had set up a US arm to bring Reebok’s professional equipment to American gyms. The
            regional stores and distributors then sell the home range country by country. In
            Australia, for example, the official Reebok Fitness store’s click-and-collect points are
            in Wetherill Park, New South Wales, and Willawong, Queensland, and the retailer Lifespan
            Fitness Australia lists Reebok as a product partner.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Before: ICON Health & Fitness</h3>
          <p>
            For much of the 2000s Reebok home equipment in the US was made by ICON Health & Fitness of
            Logan, Utah. ICON’s annual report for the year to May 2004 lists Reebok, with Weider and
            Gold’s Gym, among the trademarks it used under licence, and a 2007 safety recall of
            Reebok-branded inversion benches was issued by ICON. ICON renamed itself iFIT in 2021. That
            earlier licence is why parts suppliers keep two separate Reebok catalogues, one for
            ICON-built machines and one for later ones, and why an old Reebok in an American basement
            is often an ICON machine underneath.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why it matters to you</h3>
          <p>
            Warranty, parts and service follow the licensee and the local distributor, not the logo.
            A Reebok treadmill bought in Australia is supported by the Australian distributor; an
            ICON-era Reebok is an ICON parts question; and a Reebok bought from a US marketplace
            seller is supported by whoever that seller says it is. Always find out which before you
            buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-country" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Which Reebok Treadmills Are Sold Where?</h2>
<p>
            This is what the official sources showed on 14 October 2026. Model availability changes
            often, and many retailers outside these stores carry the range.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmills listed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Stock on the day</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Official product catalogue (global)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ERS 510T, ERS 520T, i-Run 4.5, i-Run 5, Z-Tech DC, Z-Tech AC, FR20z, FR30z</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Catalogue only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UK Reebok Fitness store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GT40z, FR30z, FR20z, Jet 200, Jet 300, Z-Tech AC</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GT40z and one FR30z colour in stock; the rest out</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Australian Reebok Fitness store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FR20z (A$1,299), FR30z (A$1,698)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In stock</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">US Reebok Fitness store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None; a step and a deck only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Both unavailable</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon US</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No Reebok treadmill in search results</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The US picture in more detail</h3>
          <p>
            Reebok’s US equipment store promotes the GT40z and the FR30z on its home page but had no
            treadmills in its product list. Search results show Walmart listings for the FR30z and the
            Jet 100z, an Academy Sports listing for a 120-volt FR30z and the Jet 100z, and a Dick’s
            Sporting Goods category page for Reebok treadmills; all three sites blocked our checks, so
            we cannot say what was in stock. An independent review site updated in May 2026 says the
            Floatride is the only Reebok model you are likely to find in stock at Amazon, Walmart or
            Target. Reebok Fitness publishes a US support number, 1-800-215-6216, which is the number
            to ask about warranty service before you buy from any US seller.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to
            <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill at Dick’s</a>
            and our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explain how to tell who is selling, shipping and supporting a listing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="i-run" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Reebok i-Run and Other “i” Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            When people search for a “Reebok i treadmill”, they usually mean the i-Run, Reebok’s
            compact folding treadmill. It has been sold in several generations, and the current
            official catalogue lists the i-Run 4.5 and i-Run 5; Costco UK has listed an i-Run 5.0.
            Earlier i-Run versions, including the original i-Run and the i-Run 4.0, still appear on
            UK and Asian retail and resale sites.
          </p>
          <p>
            The i-Run is a home walking and jogging treadmill sold mainly in Britain, Europe and Asia,
            built to fold away in a small flat. Specifications vary by generation and by listing — one
            retailer page for an older i-Run gives a 1.75 HP motor and a 14 km/h top speed, another
            for the i-Run 4.0 gives a 1.25 CHP motor and 16 km/h — and we could not find an official
            listing and an independent review agreeing on the current model’s figures, so we do not
            quote them. If you are shopping for one, check the generation number, then the deck size,
            top speed and user weight on that exact listing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Can you buy an i-Run in the US?</h3>
          <p>
            Not that we could find. Neither Reebok’s US store nor Amazon’s US catalogue showed one. An
            i-Run bought abroad will be built for 220 to 240-volt mains, and running it on a converter
            is a fault and safety risk no warranty covers. If you want an i-Run-style machine in the
            US — a compact, folding walking and jogging treadmill — our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">small-space treadmill roundup</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill picks</a>
            list machines sold and serviced here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="floatride-and-folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Floatride, GT40z and Jet Models</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">FR20z Floatride</h3>
          <p>
            The FR20z is the compact, non-folding Floatride, with cushioning zones borrowed from
            Reebok’s running-shoe marketing, power incline and Zwift support. Its agreed figures, its
            disputed warranty and the US availability problem are covered in our
            <a href="/reebok-fr20z-floatride-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">FR20z Floatride review</a>,
            so we do not repeat them here.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">FR30z Floatride</h3>
          <p>
            The FR30z is the larger Floatride. A UK retailer listing, an independent review and a US
            Walmart listing title agree on a 4.0 HP peak motor, a top speed of 20 km/h (12.4 mph) and
            15 levels of power incline, and the independent review describes it as non-folding. The
            user weight does not agree: the independent review gives 264 lb and the Walmart listing
            title 330 lb, so confirm it for the unit you are buying. On 14 October 2026 it was A$1,698
            on Australia’s Reebok Fitness store.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">GT40z and Jet folding treadmills</h3>
          <p>
            The GT40z and the Jet series are the folding Reebok treadmills. Reebok’s US store describes
            the GT40z as having a 2.0 HP motor, 0.6 to 11.2 mph and 12 levels of power incline; an
            independent review lists the same motor and incline levels but a 10 mph top speed for what
            it calls the GT40x, so the speed is unconfirmed. The Jet 100z, 200 and 300 sit in the same
            space; the Jet 200 and 300 were out of stock on the UK store, and the Jet 100z appears in
            US retailer listings we could not check.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Reebok warranties are set by the distributor in each country. The independent review
            above reports that Reebok Fitness’s site gave 10 years on the motor and two years on parts
            and labour for the Floatride, Jet and GT models, with no frame term stated; Australian and
            UK sources for the FR20z give different terms again. Get the warranty in writing from your
            seller, for your country. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            lists what to ask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="legacy-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Older Reebok Treadmills Still in Use</h2>
<p>
            Many Reebok treadmills searched for in the US are older machines. An independent review
            site lists discontinued models for which manuals are still available, including the Reebok
            One GT40S, Competitor RT 8.0, V 8.90, R 6.90 and the ZigTech 710, 910, 1410 and 1910.
            Several of the US-market models from the 2000s were ICON builds sold through mass
            retailers and sporting goods chains.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Identifying yours</h3>
          <p>
            Find the model sticker on the frame, usually near the front of the deck or at the base of
            the upright, and photograph it with the serial number. If the paperwork or sticker carries
            ICON’s name, or the model number follows ICON’s style, parts and manuals will be filed
            under ICON-built Reebok models; parts suppliers list “Reebok (Icon)” separately from later
            Reebok machines. Search by the full model number, not the name on the console.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Parts, manuals and repair</h3>
          <p>
            For ICON-era machines, iFIT (formerly ICON) and specialist parts suppliers are the route
            to parts; for later models, the Reebok Fitness catalogue site has spare-parts and product
            support pages and country support numbers. As with any older treadmill, wear parts such as
            belts and drive belts are usually available, while consoles and motor controllers run out
            first. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            covers when a repair is worth paying for, and our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers buying an old Reebok secondhand: pay little, see it run through its full speed and
            incline range, and check parts for the exact model before you pay.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database for Reebok on 14
            October 2026 and found no recall of a Reebok treadmill. It lists a December 2007 recall of
            about 22,000 NordicTrack and Reebok inversion benches by ICON, whose ankle clamp could
            release, with a free repair kit as the remedy, and a 2008 recall of Reebok exercise cycles
            for a laceration hazard. Neither affects treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checking-a-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Check a Reebok Treadmill Listing</h2>
<p>
            Because Reebok treadmills reach shops through distributors and marketplaces, the listing
            in front of you is often the only paperwork you get before the box arrives. These checks
            take ten minutes and catch most problems.
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Match the model name to the catalogue.</strong> The official Reebok Fitness
            catalogue lists the current models by name. If a listing uses a name that is not there,
            ask the seller which model it corresponds to; suffixes such as z and x change between
            markets.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the voltage.</strong> A US listing should say 120 volts, or at least
            show a US plug. If it gives 220 to 240 volts, or says nothing, ask before ordering.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the deck size, not the footprint.</strong> Some listings quote the
            machine’s overall length where the running surface should be. A figure in the high 60s or
            70s of inches is the machine; the belt is shorter. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            gives the lengths that suit walking and running.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the motor rating type.</strong> A peak figure is not comparable with a
            continuous one. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the difference.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask who provides the warranty.</strong> The answer should name a company and
            a phone number in your country. “Manufacturer’s warranty” on its own is not an answer.</li>
            <li><strong>Check delivery and returns.</strong> A Floatride weighs well over 150 lb in its
            box. Find out whether delivery is to the kerb or to your room, and who pays freight if it
            goes back.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A seller who answers all six clearly is one worth buying from. One who cannot answer the
            warranty or voltage question is a reason to choose a different machine, however good the
            price looks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Reebok Treadmills Do Well, and Less Well</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Do well</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Cushioning and feel.</strong> The Floatride models are consistently described
            by independent reviewers as comfortable underfoot, which is the line’s main selling
            point.</li>
            <li><strong>No subscription.</strong> Current Reebok treadmills work fully from their own
            consoles. The connected models link over Bluetooth to third-party apps such as Zwift and
            Kinomap, which you can take or leave; nothing is locked behind a membership.</li>
            <li><strong>Simple, compact designs.</strong> The range leans towards straightforward
            consoles and small footprints, which suits flats and spare rooms.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Do less well</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>US availability and support.</strong> With no treadmills on the official US
            store, buying and servicing one in the US depends on the retailer.</li>
            <li><strong>Inconsistent figures.</strong> Model suffixes change between markets (z and
            x), and listings disagree on speed, capacity and warranty. Confirm figures for the exact
            unit.</li>
            <li><strong>Compact decks.</strong> Several models have shorter or narrower decks than
            full-size US home treadmills, which matters for tall or fast runners; our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains the numbers.</li>
            <li><strong>Peak-only motor ratings.</strong> Most listings give peak horsepower rather
            than a continuous rating, which makes comparison with US machines harder.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="us-buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What US Buyers Should Do</h2>
<p>
            If you have your heart set on a Reebok, buy from an established US retailer, and before
            you pay confirm three things in writing: that the unit is built for 120-volt supply, who
            provides warranty service in the US and for how long on each component, and the return
            terms, including who pays freight. Calling Reebok Fitness’s US support number with the
            model and seller’s name is a sensible check.
          </p>
          <p>
            If what attracted you was the cushioned feel, our
            <a href="/best-cushioned-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioned treadmill roundup</a>
            covers US-stocked alternatives. If it was Zwift support without a subscription, see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for Zwift</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
            And for a folding machine from a manufacturer with a US service network, our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon treadmill roundup</a>
            covers Johnson Health Tech’s current home range, with each model’s stock status on the
            day we checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are in the UK or Australia, the situation reverses: Reebok treadmills are stocked
            by official stores and established retailers, and the same checks — warranty in writing,
            delivery terms, deck size against your stride — are all you need.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


